# Web (Next.js App Router)

## Versions

Next.js 16.4 and React 19.3, on Node 24 (`web/.nvmrc`). Tailwind CSS v4, configured in `app/globals.css` (`@import "tailwindcss"; @plugin "@tailwindcss/typography";`); there is no `tailwind.config.js`.

This Next is newer than most training data. Before using an API you aren't sure of, read its page in `web/node_modules/next/dist/docs/` (see `web/AGENTS.md`, which `next dev` writes and keeps). Specifics that matter here:

- `cacheComponents` and `partialPrefetching` are on (both become the default in Next 17). Nothing is cached unless it runs inside a `"use cache"` function; give every one an explicit `cacheLife(...)` and a `cacheTag(...)`. Don't use the older `fetch` options (`cache: 'force-cache'`, `next: { revalidate, tags }`) or route segment exports (`dynamic`, `revalidate`, `fetchCache`). Docs: `docs/01-app/03-api-reference/01-directives/use-cache.md` and `docs/01-app/02-guides/migrating-to-cache-components.md`.
- An error thrown inside a `"use cache"` function reaches the caller as a plain `Error` with only a `digest`: its class and fields are gone, so `instanceof ApiError` fails. Return outcomes the page branches on (`null` for a 404 or 400, or a union), and throw only for failures that should reach `error.tsx`. Throws aren't cached; returned values are. Vitest doesn't run Next's compiler, so unit tests can't catch this; the Playwright suite (`make e2e`) runs a production build and can.
- Cached functions can't read `cookies()`, `headers()`, or `searchParams`. Read those outside the cache, inside a `<Suspense>` boundary, and pass plain values in as arguments.
- `params` and `searchParams` are Promises in pages and layouts. Pass the Promise into a component wrapped in `<Suspense>` and `await` it there, so the rest of the page can prerender.
- A cached function whose arguments are known at build time runs during `next build`. Keep API calls keyed by `params` or `searchParams` (and no `generateStaticParams`), so the build doesn't need a running Go API.
- A page component that calls a cached function with no request-derived arguments (e.g. `listTags()`) must `await io()` (from `next/cache`) before the call, inside `<Suspense>`. `io()` stops only the build's prerender; prefetches still include the content. Use `connection()` only where a real request is required, such as a route handler like `sitemap.ts`. Docs: `docs/01-app/03-api-reference/04-functions/io.md`.
- A section the page can live without (like the home page's topic column) catches its API error in the component and renders nothing, instead of letting it reach `error.tsx`. Catch outside the `"use cache"` function: a returned fallback is cached, a throw isn't.
- Turbopack is the bundler for dev and build; Tailwind runs as a Turbopack CSS loader (`@tailwindcss/turbopack`), not through PostCSS.
- Formatting is Prettier with `prettier-plugin-tailwindcss` (class order); ESLint uses `eslint-config-next` with `eslint-config-prettier` last.
- Run tools through `make web-*` from the repo root (see AGENTS.md), not bare `npm`/`npx`: the Makefile puts the right Node on `PATH`.
- Unit tests are Vitest (`vitest.config.mts`, jsdom by default; it skips `e2e/`). Vitest can't render async server components; keep pages thin and test the API client and small synchronous components. Pages, caching, the auth gates and the editor in a real browser are covered by the Playwright specs in `e2e/`; AGENTS.md has their rules.
- API types come from `lib/api/schema.d.ts`, generated by `make web-types`. Never hand-write types for API responses or edit that file; change `api/openapi.yaml` and regenerate.

- Server components by default. Add `"use client"` only for state, effects, or browser APIs, and keep those components small and at the leaves.
- Fetch data in server components through the typed API client. Don't call `fetch` against the Go API ad hoc in components.
- The API client lives in one place (e.g. `web/lib/api`), with types matching the Go JSON responses. Don't use `any`.
- Caching lives in the API client's `"use cache"` functions, not in pages or components.

```tsx
// BAD: client component just to fetch
"use client";
export default function Posts() {
  const [posts, setPosts] = useState([]);
  useEffect(() => { fetch("/api/v1/posts").then(/* ... */); }, []);
}

// GOOD: server component using the typed client
import { listPosts } from "@/lib/api";

export default async function Posts() {
  const { items } = await listPosts();
  return <PostList posts={items} />;
}
```

- Search input is a client component that updates the URL query (`?q=`); the results page stays a server component reading `searchParams`.

## Admin auth

- Call `requireAdmin()` (`lib/admin/session.ts`) in every admin page and every admin server action, inside a `<Suspense>` boundary for pages. A layout check or `proxy.ts` doesn't guard a page's data or its actions.
- Never read the session anywhere else, and never trust a GitHub id from the client: it comes from the session, which carries the id GitHub verified.
- Code that reads request data must read it before branching on server config, or `next build` (where admin env is usually unset) can prerender that branch as static. `make web-build` shows it: admin routes must be ◐ (partial prerender), not ○ (static).
- Build Better Auth through `getAuth()`, never at module load: `next build` runs with `NODE_ENV=production` and no secret, where constructing it throws.
- The GitHub id is a user field with `input: true`, because Better Auth drops `input: false` fields from `mapProfileToUser`. A `databaseHooks.user.update.before` hook keeps it read-only; `lib/auth/flow.test.ts` covers that.

## Admin API calls

- Call the admin API only through `lib/api/admin.ts`, passing the `Admin` from `requireAdmin()`. Don't add `"use cache"` there or export it from the `lib/api` barrel: it's per-admin and uncached.
- Treat `updated_at` as an opaque string: pass it back exactly as loaded. Never put it through `new Date()` or `toISOString()`; that drops the microseconds and every save becomes a 409.
- Server actions take plain objects, not `FormData`, and check their argument's shape at runtime (`lib/admin/input.ts`): an action is a public POST endpoint, and TypeScript types don't reach the wire. Content rules stay in the API.
- After a write, expire the caches that render it with `updateTag`: `posts` and `post:<slug>`, plus `post:<old slug>` on a rename.

## Editor

- The editor loads only in the browser (`components/admin/post-editor-loader.tsx`, `next/dynamic` with `ssr: false`): Tiptap can't render on the server, and the publish controls show the author's local time. Keep local-time rendering inside client-only components, or the server's UTC HTML mismatches on hydration.
- Every editor extension needs a markdown form goldmark renders; `lib/admin/editor-extensions.ts` is the one list, and its test checks each toolbar feature's markdown. Underline is off for that reason. Tiptap packages are pinned to one exact version (their peer dependencies require it); upgrade them together.
- Next keeps up to three visited routes mounted but hidden (`<Activity>`), with their state. Forms that create something must reset after success when hidden (`useLayoutEffect` cleanup, as `PostEditor` does), or revisiting the page shows a form still bound to the created record. See `docs/01-app/02-guides/preserving-ui-state.md`.
- Tiptap's markdown output escapes more than needed (`&amp;`, `\*`, `\_`) and drops inline raw HTML tags. Goldmark renders the same HTML, and Postgres's parser ignores `&amp;` (an `entity` token) and the backslashes, so search is unaffected.
